Nice fix — the UUID-based identity approach is the right call here.

A few observations from review:

**The fix is broader than advertised**
The PR migrates all stateful modules (`ExtractionModule`, `MergerModule`, `ProcessModule`, `CraftingModule`, `ProviderModule`) from `ctx.moduleState(getStateKey())` to `ctx.moduleState(this)`, which means the state isolation fix applies to all of them through the scoped context wired up in `ChassisPipe.getDynamicModuleEntries`. That's great — this bug would have affected any stateful module that can be placed twice in the same chassis. Would be worth updating the PR description and/or adding a test case for one of the other modules (e.g. two `ExtractionModule`s) to make the broader coverage explicit.

**Logic duplication between `getDynamicModuleEntries` and `OpenChassisSlotPacket`**
Both have identical blocks for detecting a missing module ID and re-encoding the stack:
```java
boolean missingModuleId = ModuleItem.getModuleId(stack).isBlank();
// ... create module, get stateKey ...
if (missingModuleId) {
    ItemStack.CODEC.encodeStart(...).result().ifPresent(...);
    ctx.markDirtyAndSync();
}
```
If this logic needs to change, it has to be updated in two places. Worth extracting into a helper — something like `ChassisPipe.getOrAssignModuleStateKey(ctx, slotIndex, ops)`.

**`IdentityHashMap` on `Module` instances needs a comment**
`PipeContext.withModuleStateKey` uses `IdentityHashMap<Module, String>`, which relies on object identity rather than equality. This is correct because each `Module` is a distinct instance from `moduleItem.createModule()`, and the exact instance is threaded from `getDynamicModuleEntries` through to the `moduleState(module)` lookup. But this invariant is non-obvious — if `getDynamicModuleEntries` is ever called twice for the same slot, the two calls produce different instances and the map lookup silently falls back to the class-keyed default. A short comment on `withModuleStateKey` explaining the identity requirement would prevent a future footgun.

**`@Nullable` on `targetModuleStateKey` constructor param**
In `SupplierScreenHandler`, the field is declared `@Nullable private final String targetModuleStateKey` but the constructor parameter of the same name is missing the annotation. Minor, but inconsistent with the rest of the file.

